/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 69 0 R
/T (For much of the 20th century, global security, conflict resolution, and environmental policy were treated as gender-neutral fields. Major international treaties and peace settlements operated under the assumption that political stability and military security automatically benefited all citizens equally, completely ignoring how war, displacement, and resource scarcity uniquely impact women. The turning point occurred in October 2000, when the UN Security Council unanimously passed Resolution 1325.)
/E (For much of the 20th century, global security, conflict resolution, and environmental policy were treated as gender-neutral fields. Major international treaties and peace settlements operated under the assumption that political stability and military security automatically benefited all citizens equally, completely ignoring how war, displacement, and resource scarcity uniquely impact women. The turning point occurred in October 2000, when the UN Security Council unanimously passed Resolution 1325.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
211 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 69 0 R
/T ( This groundbreaking resolution formally created the Women, Peace, and Security \(WPS\) Agenda, marking the first time the international community officially acknowledged that sustainable global peace is mathematically impossible to achieve without the active, equal participation of women in conflict prevention, peace negotiations, and post-conflict governance.)
/E ( This groundbreaking resolution formally created the Women, Peace, and Security \(WPS\) Agenda, marking the first time the international community officially acknowledged that sustainable global peace is mathematically impossible to achieve without the active, equal participation of women in conflict prevention, peace negotiations, and post-conflict governance.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
212 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 69 0 R
/T (This framework was further reinforced over the following decades through international climate agreements, most notably the 2015 Paris Agreement, which explicitly recognized that climate change disproportionately affects vulnerable populations and called on nations to adopt gender-responsive climate adaptation plans. Modern academic research and diplomatic data have consistently validated these initiatives, demonstrating that peace agreements signed with female negotiators and local women's civil society groups are 35% more likely to last at least fifteen years compared to those drafted exclusively by male military or political elites. Despite these clear historical mandates and empirical findings, implementation across member states has been notoriously sluggish. Over the past twenty-five years, women have still accounted for fewer than 10% of negotiators in major diplomatic peace tracks globally, revealing a persistent and dangerous gap between international commitments and real-world execution.)
/E (This framework was further reinforced over the following decades through international climate agreements, most notably the 2015 Paris Agreement, which explicitly recognized that climate change disproportionately affects vulnerable populations and called on nations to adopt gender-responsive climate adaptation plans. Modern academic research and diplomatic data have consistently validated these initiatives, demonstrating that peace agreements signed with female negotiators and local women's civil society groups are 35% more likely to last at least fifteen years compared to those drafted exclusively by male military or political elites. Despite these clear historical mandates and empirical findings, implementation across member states has been notoriously sluggish. Over the past twenty-five years, women have still accounted for fewer than 10% of negotiators in major diplomatic peace tracks globally, revealing a persistent and dangerous gap between international commitments and real-world execution.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
213 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 69 0 R
/T (When armed conflicts break out or climate disasters devastate communities, standard physical and social infrastructures collapse rapidly. In these volatile environments, basic survival often falls squarely onto the shoulders of women, who take on the burden of securing clean water, gathering scarce food supplies, managing healthcare needs, and maintaining community cohesion. Despite performing these critical roles on the ground, women are almost entirely excluded when international organizations, peace negotiators, and local governments gather to design post-crisis recovery policies, allocate humanitarian funding, or restructure governance.)
/E (When armed conflicts break out or climate disasters devastate communities, standard physical and social infrastructures collapse rapidly. In these volatile environments, basic survival often falls squarely onto the shoulders of women, who take on the burden of securing clean water, gathering scarce food supplies, managing healthcare needs, and maintaining community cohesion. Despite performing these critical roles on the ground, women are almost entirely excluded when international organizations, peace negotiators, and local governments gather to design post-crisis recovery policies, allocate humanitarian funding, or restructure governance.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
