215 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 69 0 R
/T (Implementing "gender-responsive strategies" requires a fundamental shift in how the international community approaches crisis management. Rather than simply delivering short-term humanitarian assistance, gender-responsive policymaking intentionally addresses the underlying structural inequalities that make women more vulnerable to crises in the first place. This means restructuring legal systems to guarantee women equal land ownership, establishing financial channels that grant female entrepreneurs credit after a collapse, and creating formal seats for women at governance tables during crisis recovery. When policies actively empower women to direct resource management and community stabilization, entire regions demonstrate significantly higher resilience, lower rates of civil unrest, and more equitable economic recovery following major shocks.)
/E (Implementing "gender-responsive strategies" requires a fundamental shift in how the international community approaches crisis management. Rather than simply delivering short-term humanitarian assistance, gender-responsive policymaking intentionally addresses the underlying structural inequalities that make women more vulnerable to crises in the first place. This means restructuring legal systems to guarantee women equal land ownership, establishing financial channels that grant female entrepreneurs credit after a collapse, and creating formal seats for women at governance tables during crisis recovery. When policies actively empower women to direct resource management and community stabilization, entire regions demonstrate significantly higher resilience, lower rates of civil unrest, and more equitable economic recovery following major shocks.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
