1031 0 obj
<<
/Title (For much of the 20th century, global security, conflict resolution, and environmental policy were treated as gender-neutral fields. Major international treaties and peace settlements operated under the assumption that political stability and military security automatically benefited all citizens equally, completely ignoring how war, displacement, and resource scarcity uniquely impact women. The turning point occurred in October 2000, when the UN Security Council unanimously passed Resolution 1325.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 961 0 R
/Next 1059 0 R
>>
endobj
1032 0 obj
<<
/Title (This framework was further reinforced over the following decades through international climate agreements, most notably the 2015 Paris Agreement, which explicitly recognized that climate change disproportionately affects vulnerable populations and called on nations to adopt gender-responsive climate adaptation plans. Modern academic research and diplomatic data have consistently validated these initiatives, demonstrating that peace agreements signed with female negotiators and local women's civil society groups are 35% more likely to last at least fifteen years compared to those drafted exclusively by male military or political elites. Despite these clear historical mandates and empirical findings, implementation across member states has been notoriously sluggish. Over the past twenty-five years, women have still accounted for fewer than 10% of negotiators in major diplomatic peace tracks globally, revealing a persistent and dangerous gap between international commitments and real-world execution.)
/Dest [15 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 961 0 R
/Prev 1059 0 R
>>
endobj
1033 0 obj
<<
/Title (Following conflict, women play a vital role in rebuilding communities and promoting lasting peace. Delegates should explore how women can be meaningfully included in peace negotiations, governance, and decision-making processes to ensure recovery efforts are inclusive and sustainable. .)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 962 0 R
>>
endobj
